WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.590 --> 00:00:04.880
The story of Cesar Millan is often seen as
a typical rags to riches tale about a young

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:08.340
man from Mexico crossing the border in pursuit
of the American dream.

00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:12.920
While that's more or less how things happened,
the reality of Millan's eventful life is a

00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:14.440
little more complicated.

00:00:14.540 --> 00:00:19.500
While Millan became known to millions of viewers
worldwide as the Dog Whisperer, his off-camera

00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:21.360
life has been anything but quiet.

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:26.300
He was bullied for his love of dogs

00:00:26.780 --> 00:00:30.960
Millan grew up on his grandfather's farm in
Sinaloa, in northwestern Mexico.

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:34.900
During this time he became accustomed to working
with a variety of animals, though he claims

00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:37.860
to have had a "natural way with dogs" from
a very young age.

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:41.980
This connection with canines was so strong
that MIllan found himself being followed by

00:00:41.980 --> 00:00:46.700
dogs every time he left his home, a fact that
didn't go unnoticed by the other kids.

00:00:46.890 --> 00:00:51.560
He was constantly teased by his peers, who
started calling him El Perrero, which, according

00:00:51.560 --> 00:00:54.560
to Millan, roughly translates to "the Dirty
Dog Boy."

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:58.120
Millan refused to let bullying change his
mind about his love of dogs.

00:00:58.240 --> 00:01:02.140
Instead, he concentrated on making his dream
a reality from a young age.

00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:06.600
By the time he was 13 years old he had laid
out plans to become the best dog trainer in

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:10.940
the world — and he'd decided that the best
place to begin his takeover was the US.

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:15.760
Inspired by the immaculately well-trained
performing dogs he saw in TV reruns of Lassie

00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:19.799
and The Adventures of Rin Tin Tin, Millan
set his sights on Hollywood, convinced his

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:21.440
talents would be most welcome there.

00:01:22.340 --> 00:01:23.840
He entered the US illegally

00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:29.560
In 1991, Millan made his way from his home
to a Tijuana bus station with $100 to his

00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:31.810
name, seeking transportation across the border.

00:01:31.810 --> 00:01:36.490
When he finally found a way out of Mexico
via a hole in the border fence, he was forced

00:01:36.490 --> 00:01:40.480
to pay $100 — every cent he had — in order
to pass through.

00:01:40.590 --> 00:01:45.060
Millan entered the United States with no money,
no friends, little to no knowledge of the

00:01:45.080 --> 00:01:47.040
English language, and nowhere to live.

00:01:47.270 --> 00:01:51.560
He spent his early days in America living
homeless on the streets of San Diego, sleeping

00:01:51.579 --> 00:01:55.479
under a freeway and surviving on nothing but
convenience store hot dogs.

00:01:55.479 --> 00:01:59.609
Millan recalled that he could get two hot
dogs for 99 cents at the time, meaning all

00:01:59.609 --> 00:02:02.340
he had to do to survive was somehow make a
dollar a day.

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:06.360
Many hot dogs later, he managed to land his
first real job Stateside.

00:02:07.220 --> 00:02:08.780
He found work as a dog groomer

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:11.720
Using the first English sentence he ever learned
…

00:02:11.720 --> 00:02:14.300
"I learned to say "do you have application
for work?"

00:02:14.540 --> 00:02:16.500
That was the first sentence I'm gonna learn."

00:02:16.600 --> 00:02:20.460
… Millan managed to arrange some part-time
hours in a local dog groomer's office.

00:02:20.660 --> 00:02:24.860
The owners were instantly impressed with his
assertive handling of larger, aggressive dogs

00:02:24.860 --> 00:02:26.900
that had previously been impossible to control.

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:31.900
Millan saw the value in this and, after moving
to Inglewood, decided to start charging for

00:02:31.900 --> 00:02:33.140
a group dog-walking service.

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:38.260
He quickly attracted attention with his unusual
— and very much illegal — style of walking

00:02:38.260 --> 00:02:43.200
his group off leash, quickly earning a reputation
as "the Mexican guy who can walk a pack of

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:43.700
dogs."

00:02:43.700 --> 00:02:47.620
Some of his first clients were Will and Jada
Pinkett Smith, who paid for Millan to have

00:02:47.620 --> 00:02:49.300
a year of English language lessons.

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:51.520
His reputation grew quickly

00:02:52.060 --> 00:02:56.720
Millan and his dog-walking business spread
like wildfire by word of mouth, and he eventually

00:02:56.720 --> 00:03:00.940
earned enough money to open his Dog Psychology
Center in South Central Los Angeles.

00:03:00.980 --> 00:03:06.160
His success at rehabilitating dogs with particularly
troublesome pasts lead to him being profiled

00:03:06.160 --> 00:03:09.940
in a 2002 edition of the L.A. Times, and the
reaction was huge.

00:03:10.109 --> 00:03:14.420
The story led a string of producers to his
door hoping to create a new TV show highlighting

00:03:14.420 --> 00:03:18.579
Millan's techniques, and the trainer signed
on to front Dog Whisperer with Cesar Millan

00:03:18.580 --> 00:03:19.100
soon after.

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:24.020
The National Geographic Channel ended up picking
up the show for 26 episodes after Animal Planet

00:03:24.020 --> 00:03:25.500
refused to commit beyond a pilot.

00:03:26.020 --> 00:03:28.260
He has been involved in a number of lawsuits

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:31.380
Things weren’t all sunshine and rainbows
for Millan, however.

00:03:34.500 --> 00:03:35.240
" … it's alright, it's alright."

00:03:35.520 --> 00:03:40.340
The Dog Whisperer star was the subject of
two lawsuits in a single week in 2006, the

00:03:40.340 --> 00:03:44.640
first involving his former publicist Makeda
Smith and her partner Foster Corder.

00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:48.500
The couple claimed that they had come up with
the name "Dog Whisperer" while in discussion

00:03:48.500 --> 00:03:49.140
with Millan.

00:03:49.340 --> 00:03:53.499
After they received no recognition of their
contribution they decided to sue the National

00:03:53.499 --> 00:03:56.879
Geographic Channel, asking for $5 million
in damages.

00:03:56.879 --> 00:04:02.239
Millan was in hot water again days later when
8 Simple Rules producer Flody Suarez claimed

00:04:02.239 --> 00:04:06.500
that his five-year-old Labrador retriever
Gator was subjected to inhumane treatment

00:04:06.500 --> 00:04:08.260
at the celebrity trainer's facility.

00:04:08.380 --> 00:04:12.999
Suarez's lawsuit stated that Gator was covered
with bruises and gasping for breath after

00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:14.760
being forced to run on a treadmill.

00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:19.880
The producer went on to describe how a choke
chain had caused damage to Gator's esophagus

00:04:19.880 --> 00:04:25.660
and that surgery was needed to correct it,
with the vet's bills totaling more than $25,000.

00:04:26.040 --> 00:04:27.940
He has been accused of animal cruelty

00:04:28.380 --> 00:04:34.760
"Cesar Millan … AKA the dog whisperer, is
under fire, for of all things, animal cruelty."

00:04:35.020 --> 00:04:39.580
The incident with Gator the Labrador is not
the only allegation of animal cruelty Millan

00:04:39.580 --> 00:04:40.080
has faced.

00:04:40.120 --> 00:04:45.000
American Humane, the oldest national organization
specializing in the protection of animals,

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:49.140
revealed they had sent a letter to the National
Geographic Channel in 2006.

00:04:49.140 --> 00:04:54.040
The letter in question expressed outrage about
the "inhumane" and "potentially harmful" tactics

00:04:54.040 --> 00:04:56.540
employed on Dog Whisperer with Cesar Millan.

00:04:56.620 --> 00:05:01.020
Other critics of Millan's methods include
certified animal behavior consultant and pet

00:05:01.020 --> 00:05:05.090
journalist Steve Dale, who refers to Millan
as the "Dog Screamer," along with British

00:05:05.090 --> 00:05:09.889
television personality Alan Titchmarsh, who
berated Millan over his "barbaric" techniques

00:05:09.889 --> 00:05:12.440
during an interview on his afternoon TV show.

00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:14.960
He hit hard times in 2010

00:05:15.160 --> 00:05:19.940
2010 was a terrible year for Millan, starting
off in the worst possible way when his sidekick

00:05:19.940 --> 00:05:22.320
Daddy died of cancer at the age of 16.

00:05:22.740 --> 00:05:24.419
"He helped me for 16 years.

00:05:25.480 --> 00:05:28.300
You know I don't think I did much for him,
as much as he did for me."

00:05:28.420 --> 00:05:32.900
The gentle giant, whom he'd adopted from rapper
Redman, was not just Millan's assistant on

00:05:32.900 --> 00:05:35.120
the show — he was his best friend off camera.

00:05:35.120 --> 00:05:39.340
Sadly, Milan barely had time to mourn the
loss when he was hit with more bad news.

00:05:39.340 --> 00:05:43.639
The following month, his wife of 16 years
informed him she had filed for divorce.

00:05:43.639 --> 00:05:48.110
Millan was forced to assess his finances,
and that was when the third blow came: He

00:05:48.110 --> 00:05:52.540
soon realized that, despite being one of the
most recognizable TV personalities in America,

00:05:52.620 --> 00:05:56.880
he'd made a series of bad business decisions
that had left him with little to show for

00:05:56.880 --> 00:05:58.060
his nationwide fame.

00:05:58.920 --> 00:06:00.100
He tried to end it all

00:06:00.700 --> 00:06:05.960
In 2012, Millan opened up about the full extent
of what he'd gone through two years earlier.

00:06:05.960 --> 00:06:10.840
He claimed he'd lost his "source of calmness"
when his beloved dog Daddy died, and the added

00:06:10.840 --> 00:06:15.180
pressure of his divorce and financial woes
sent him into a downward spiral of depression.

00:06:15.449 --> 00:06:19.080
The famous trainer got to the point where
he didn't want to be alive anymore, taking

00:06:19.080 --> 00:06:22.400
what he described as "a whole bunch of pills"
and hoping not to wake up.

00:06:22.580 --> 00:06:26.440
Fortunately for Millan, his sons discovered
him before it was too late and he was rushed

00:06:26.440 --> 00:06:27.200
to the hospital.

00:06:27.449 --> 00:06:31.580
He later talked about how regaining consciousness
in a ward full of patients who loved his show

00:06:31.580 --> 00:06:33.380
helped him to get back on track with his life.

00:06:34.280 --> 00:06:36.120
He used his own methods to bounce back

00:06:36.640 --> 00:06:40.800
Millan was offered antidepressants after he
failed to take his own life, though he decided

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:43.300
that the best way to heal was to practice
what he preached.

00:06:43.380 --> 00:06:48.460
In place of pills, Millan devised a rehabilitation
schedule based on his knowledge of pack life,

00:06:48.460 --> 00:06:52.600
using exercise, discipline and affection to
conquer his depression — and it seems to

00:06:52.600 --> 00:06:53.720
have worked wonders.

00:06:53.720 --> 00:06:57.940
His path led him to new partner Jahira Dar
— whom Millan credits as being instrumental

00:06:57.940 --> 00:07:01.780
in his recovery — along with a series of
new opportunities in television.

00:07:01.880 --> 00:07:06.280
He was soon back on small screens everywhere
in Cesar Millan's The Leader of the Pack and

00:07:06.280 --> 00:07:07.360
Cesar 911.

00:07:07.610 --> 00:07:12.180
He's even branched out to younger audiences,
working alongside his son Calvin in Nickelodeon's

00:07:12.180 --> 00:07:13.980
live action series Mutt and Stuff.

00:07:14.550 --> 00:07:15.540
Thanks for watching!

